/// Rebuild NearbyEnemies for all living units using brute-force O(n²) distance checks.
/// Sets `attacking` flag on units that moved this tick (flags.moved is set by movement).
pub fn run(world: &mut hecs::World) {
    // 1. Collect all living unit data in one query
    let units: Vec<(Entity, UnitPosition, String)> = world
        .query::<(Entity, &UnitPosition, &UnitOwner)>()
        .iter()
        .map(|(e, pos, owner)| (e, *pos, owner.player_id.clone()))
        .collect();

    // 2. For each unit, find enemies within COMBAT_RANGE
    for i in 0..units.len() {
        let enemies: Vec<Entity> = (0..units.len())
            .filter(|&j| {
                i != j && units[i].2 != units[j].2  // different owner
                    && dist_sq(units[i].1, units[j].1) < COMBAT_RANGE_SQ
            })
            .map(|j| units[j].0)
            .collect();

        // 3. Write NearbyEnemies (create or update)
        // 4. Set attacking flag if unit moved
    }
}
```

Key design decisions:
- No scratch buffer needed — the O(n²) vec is small enough to allocate per-tick at game scale
- `initialized` flag on NearbyEnemies is always set to `true` (no more lazy init)
- `attacking` flag is set for any unit with `flags.moved == true` that has enemies (matches current spatialty behavior)
- No `world_entity` parameter needed (unlike spatialty which recorded debug timings)

